        <div><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-dark">GOOD TO KNOW</p><h2>Local SEO checklist <span class="accent-text">FAQs</span></h2><p class="faq-lead">A few important points before you start improving your local search presence.</p></div>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details><summary>What is a local SEO checklist?</summary><p>A local SEO checklist is a structured way to review the website and online presence signals that can help a business appear in relevant local search results. It commonly covers Google Business Profile, website content, technical accessibility, reviews and local references.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Does completing this checklist guarantee higher Google rankings?</summary><p>No. Search visibility depends on many factors, including relevance, distance and prominence for local results, as well as competition and search context. This checklist helps identify issues and improvement opportunities; it cannot guarantee rankings, traffic or leads.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How often should I perform a local SEO audit?</summary><p>Review important business information whenever it changes. A broader check every three to six months is a sensible starting point, with additional reviews after a website migration, redesign, major content update or change in business locations.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Do I need paid SEO tools?</summary><p>No. Many checks can be completed with publicly available pages and free tools such as Google Business Profile, Google Search Console, PageSpeed Insights and Rich Results Test. Some advanced crawling and competitive research tasks may benefit from paid tools, but they are not required to use this checklist.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What should I fix first?</summary><p>Start with issues that prevent customers or search engines from finding accurate information: incorrect business details, an unavailable or blocked website, important pages accidentally set to noindex, broken redirects, or incorrect canonical URLs. Then work through relevance, content, reputation and measurement improvements.</p></details>
        </div>
